1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:08,284
Welcome to Episode Fourteen, the final episode of Level Seven: FCPO practical case studies.

2
00:00:08,704 --> 00:00:17,944
We apply every Level Seven concept to crude palm oil futures on Bursa Malaysia Derivatives, without transferring forex assumptions mechanically.

3
00:00:18,364 --> 00:00:27,556
Every chart in this episode is an illustrative scenario, not actual historical trading data. No entries, stops or profit claims are given.

4
00:00:29,376 --> 00:00:36,072
First, the reference specifications. One FCPO contract is twenty-five metric tonnes.

5
00:00:36,492 --> 00:00:43,356
The minimum price fluctuation is one ringgit per tonne, so one tick is worth twenty-five ringgit per contract.

6
00:00:43,776 --> 00:00:55,392
Trading hours are ten thirty to twelve thirty and two thirty to six p m, Monday to Friday, plus the T plus one session from nine to eleven thirty p m, Monday to Thursday.

7
00:00:55,812 --> 00:01:00,588
Contract months and delivery rules are set out in Bursa’s specification.

8
00:01:01,008 --> 00:01:05,856
Always verify these against Bursa’s current documentation before using them.

9
00:01:07,676 --> 00:01:15,284
The bullish case. On the daily FCPO chart, structure is bullish, with higher highs and higher lows.

10
00:01:15,704 --> 00:01:19,592
The potential draw is buy-side liquidity above the old high.

11
00:01:20,012 --> 00:01:24,788
Price is retracing into discount, near a bullish fair value gap.

12
00:01:25,208 --> 00:01:34,904
So the Daily Bias is bullish, as a working hypothesis, and today’s realistic objective is the previous day high at four thousand two hundred and forty-four.

13
00:01:36,724 --> 00:01:44,836
On the fifteen-minute chart, the day opens at ten thirty. The morning session forms a range around the open: accumulation.

14
00:01:45,256 --> 00:01:49,600
The morning low, touched twice, becomes the sell-side reference.

15
00:01:50,020 --> 00:01:59,116
After the lunch break, the afternoon session opens with a drop that sweeps the morning low. That is the manipulation phase in PO3 terms.

16
00:01:59,536 --> 00:02:12,184
Could it be called a Judas Swing? Only as a Judas-style interpretation that you have tested on FCPO. ICT defined the concept around New York and London opens, not Bursa sessions.

17
00:02:12,604 --> 00:02:16,396
Bullish displacement follows, leaving a fair value gap.

18
00:02:16,816 --> 00:02:18,952
Price breaks above the morning high.

19
00:02:19,372 --> 00:02:25,204
Distribution carries price to the previous day high, where buy-side liquidity is taken.

20
00:02:27,024 --> 00:02:33,720
And the alternative. The afternoon sweep simply continues lower. No reclaim, no displacement.

21
00:02:34,140 --> 00:02:40,716
Price breaks the invalidation level, written before the session. The bullish interpretation is abandoned.

22
00:02:41,136 --> 00:02:48,336
Every CMI case is summarised in a case card: context, timeframe, bias and liquidity.

23
00:02:48,756 --> 00:02:53,652
Session timing, and the PO3 or AMD interpretation.

24
00:02:54,072 --> 00:02:58,680
Judas and SMT status, structure and PD arrays.

25
00:02:59,100 --> 00:03:02,724
And, always, invalidation and the alternative.

26
00:03:04,544 --> 00:03:15,320
The bearish case mirrors it. The daily structure is bearish, price has retraced into premium near a bearish fair value gap, and the draw is sell-side liquidity below.

27
00:03:15,740 --> 00:03:22,556
Intraday, the morning session ranges around the ten thirty open. The morning high is the buy-side reference.

28
00:03:22,976 --> 00:03:31,400
The afternoon open sweeps the morning high, a possible Judas-style move, only if your testing on FCPO supports that reading.

29
00:03:31,820 --> 00:03:37,148
Bearish displacement leaves a fair value gap, and price breaks below the morning low.

30
00:03:37,568 --> 00:03:46,472
Distribution runs toward the previous day low. The alternative, where price accepts above the swept high, invalidates the bearish case.

31
00:03:48,292 --> 00:04:00,220
For FCPO session analysis, start from Bursa’s sessions: morning, afternoon and the T plus one session. Mark each session’s open, high and low as references.

32
00:04:00,640 --> 00:04:07,264
Remember that T plus one trades are booked to the next trading day, which affects how daily candles are built.

33
00:04:07,684 --> 00:04:17,980
ICT Kill Zones are defined in New York time. The London window overlaps the FCPO afternoon on the clock, but that is coincidence, not a definition.

34
00:04:18,400 --> 00:04:32,416
International influence is real: Dalian oils, CBOT soybean oil, the ringgit, crude oil and export data. These can explain FCPO moves without any ICT model.

35
00:04:32,836 --> 00:04:41,236
Local FCPO price behaviour is the final judge. Test any time-based idea on FCPO data itself.

36
00:04:43,056 --> 00:04:50,000
If you compare FCPO with Dalian or CBOT, verify the actual contracts and their trading sessions.

37
00:04:51,420 --> 00:04:56,916
Synchronise the timestamps to Malaysia time, and explain the economic relationship.

38
00:04:57,336 --> 00:05:03,696
Explain the currency and contract differences, and use actual data only if it is reliable.

39
00:05:04,116 --> 00:05:12,660
Label any illustrative example clearly, as in Episode Twelve. And never fabricate a historical SMT divergence.

40
00:05:14,480 --> 00:05:20,648
That completes Level Seven. Power of Three and AMD describe how price may be delivered.

41
00:05:21,068 --> 00:05:26,252
Kill Zones and Daily Bias tell us when to watch, and which direction we expect.

42
00:05:26,672 --> 00:05:33,272
Judas Swing and SMT are refined interpretations that require context and confirmation.

43
00:05:33,692 --> 00:05:39,620
Your next step is the Level Seven comprehensive assessment. Thank you, and trade with discipline.

44
00:05:41,440 --> 00:05:47,176
Time to check your understanding. Pause the video and answer these five questions in your own words.

45
00:05:49,096 --> 00:05:54,520
Write down your reasoning, not just a one-word answer. The reasoning is what we assess.

46
00:05:58,340 --> 00:06:04,556
Here are the short answers. Compare them with your reasoning; the workbook explains each one in full.

47
